-- title:
--   Lemma 3.6: Central quadratic cone is a recession direction cone
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   In an admissible structural presentation write $x=x_z+x_V+x_l$ and define the closed cone $C_{x,z}$ generated by $[v,[v,x_l]]$, for $v\in V$. Then
--
--   $$C_{x,z}\subseteq\operatorname{lim}(\operatorname{co}(x))\cap\mathfrak z.$$
--
--   The recession cone is computed in the ambient algebra. This identifies central recession directions of the orbit hull.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 9, Lemma 3.6: Central quadratic cone is a recession direction cone

